      "verdict": "curriculum ordering hurts on a single pass",
      "diagnosis": "Difficulty is not independent of the label: 41% of its variance is explained by the class. Sorting by difficulty therefore partially sorts by class, and a single pass of SGD over class-blocked data ends fitted to whatever it saw last. The ordering is not teaching the model an easier version of the problem first; it is removing the shuffling that stops recency bias.",
      "caveat": "One pass over a linear model is the regime where ordering can matter. It is evidence about the curriculum, not about the QLoRA fine-tune, which is not run here."
